  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "can: ability and possibility",
      "title_form": "Can: habilidad y posibilidad",
      "title_target": "Can: ability and possibility",
      "description_md_form": "**Can** es un verbo modal que usamos para hablar de **habilidad** (saber hacer algo) y **posibilidad** (poder hacer algo). Es muy fácil porque **no cambia** con la persona: *I can*, *you can*, *he can*, *we can*, *they can*.\n\n**Estructura:**\n- **Afirmativo:** Sujeto + **can** + verbo base → *We can reuse bottles.*\n- **Negativo:** Sujeto + **can't** + verbo base → *We can't throw away plastic.*\n- **Pregunta:** **Can** + sujeto + verbo base? → *Can we save energy?*\n\n**Can** siempre va seguido del verbo en su forma base, sin *to* y sin *-s*. Por ejemplo: *She can reduce pollution* (no *she cans*).\n\nUsamos **can** para hablar de acciones simples que ayudan al medio ambiente: *We can reuse old boxes*, *We can save energy*.",
      "description_md_target": "**Can** is a modal verb we use to talk about **ability** (knowing how to do something) and **possibility** (being able to do something). It is very easy because it **does not change** with the person: *I can*, *you can*, *he can*, *we can*, *they can*.\n\n**Structure:**\n- **Affirmative:** Subject + **can** + base verb → *We can reuse bottles.*\n- **Negative:** Subject + **can't** + base verb → *We can't throw away plastic.*\n- **Question:** **Can** + subject + base verb? → *Can we save energy?*\n\n**Can** is always followed by the base form of the verb, without *to* and without *-s*. For example: *She can reduce pollution* (not *she cans*).\n\nWe use **can** to talk about simple actions that help the environment: *We can reuse old boxes*, *We can save energy*.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + can + base verb",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "can_modal",
        "headers": [
          "Pronombre",
          "Afirmativo",
          "Negativo",
          "Pregunta"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "I",
            "I can",
            "I can't",
            "Can I?"
          ],
          [
            "you",
            "you can",
            "you can't",
            "Can you?"
          ],
          [
            "he/she/it",
            "he can",
            "he can't",
            "Can he?"
          ],
          [
            "we",
            "we can",
            "we can't",
            "Can we?"
          ],
          [
            "they",
            "they can",
            "they can't",
            "Can they?"
          ]
        ]
      },
